# concierge.fayth — the Concierge persona: the operator's own session, and the phone's way
# in. It is the only persona in this chamber that NOTHING may summon except a human.
#
# A fayth is a persona DEFINITION; an aeon is one summoned instance of it. The other five
# define a worker: a predicate carving a partition out of the graph, the memories it must
# read, the tools it may use, and what wakes it. This one defines an OPERATOR, and three of
# those four still apply — the memories, the tools, and the brief. Only the partition does
# not, because the concierge claims nothing.
#
# WHY IT IS A PERSONA AT ALL, rather than a shell script that runs `claude`
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It was the latter, and the difference was the defect. An aeon is summoned with its brief,
# its statute book and its model resolved from a file somebody maintains; the operator's own
# session got a working directory and nothing else. The operator, 2026-09-12: "when we spin
# up aeons, they come with a system prompt and a set of overlays. you don't. i think that's
# the defect here."
#
# It was not a matter of degree. `render_memories` — the function that renders `law-`
# memories in full at summon — is called from `aeon.sh` and from nowhere else, so the claim
# in brain's CLAUDE.md that the harness "injects a `# Memories in force` section into every
# agent session at summon" was true of aeons and false of every session the operator sat in
# front of. The statute that the harness checkout is production had been in force for weeks
# and had never once been delivered to the session that kept violating it (sp-u4x).
#
# So the concierge is composed the way an aeon is, out of this file, by concierge.sh.

FAYTH_NAME=concierge
FAYTH_DESC="Concierge — the operator's session: unbounded remit, reachable from the phone, claims nothing"

# NOTHING SUMMONS THIS. Not the sentinel's pool, not a lane, not the watchtower. `auto` is
# the default and every other persona takes it; `operator` means a human starts it, which
# in practice means `concierge.sh start` or the operator's own terminal.
#
# DECLARED RATHER THAN ARRANGED. Every other property that would keep this out of the pool
# does so as a SIDE EFFECT — no FAYTH_LABELS, no FAYTH_LANE — and a side effect is exactly
# what somebody changes while meaning something else. See spira_operator_fayths.
FAYTH_SUMMON=operator

# NO PARTITION, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE POINT. The concierge reaches Spira through beads it
# FILES, never through beads it claims: it is not a second control plane, and a bead it held
# would be a bead no aeon could work while it sat in a session that runs for days.
#
# fayth_partitions and the cockpit's partition map both skip a fayth with no FAYTH_LABELS,
# so leaving these empty is what keeps the concierge out of every predicate, every readiness
# count and every `unclaimable` verdict. fayth_exclude still reserves `fayth:concierge`
# against the other personas, which is correct and inert: nothing writes that label.
FAYTH_LABELS=""
FAYTH_EXCLUDE_LABELS=""

# THE STATUTE BOOK, IN FULL, AT START. This is the line the whole file exists for. `law-`
# only: SOPs are how to fix a production event and Ops is the persona that executes one, so
# a growing shelf of runbooks does not get charged to a session that will never run a
# runbook — the same split builder.fayth makes, for the same reason.
FAYTH_MEMORY_PREFIXES="law-"

# AND THE ONES IT NEEDS IN FULL, which is a different list from every worker's.
#
# render_memories renders a CORE SET in complete text and the remaining ~115 statutes as an
# index of slugs to be read on request. That split is a context budget and it is correct; what
# was wrong is that the shipped core is builder-shaped — testing, gates, landing, guards — so
# an operator session read none of the statutes that actually bind it. `law-the-harness-
# checkout-is-production` was outside the core set for the whole of the day the operator's own
# session violated it thirty-nine turns running (sp-u4x): even delivered, it would have arrived
# as a slug.
#
# These are the ones whose violation by THIS role is expensive, in the order they bite:
# where work goes, how a decision reaches him, what "done" means, and which trees are live.
FAYTH_STATUTE_CORE="law-filed-bead-queued-xor-escalated,law-no-filing-only-escalation,law-escalate-decisions-not-problems,law-decisions-surface-immediately,law-escalations-carry-their-evidence,law-answers-need-a-delivery-path,law-commit-to-a-recommendation,law-decompose-by-deliverable,law-file-it-and-let-the-loop-fix-it,law-closed-is-not-landed,law-the-harness-checkout-is-production,law-production-is-not-a-working-tree,law-prod-is-a-different-checkout,law-commit-only-paths-you-changed,law-git-dash-c-not-cd,law-absence-needs-a-positive-control,law-alerts-must-be-actionable,law-detection-outranks-rejection,law-guard-binds-the-caller,law-harness-ships-mechanism-not-inventory"

# OPUS, AND THE REMIT IS WHY. "Its remit is unbounded" (per the operator, 2026-08-30): this
# is the session that works a design out, reads across seven repositories, decides what to
# escalate and what to decompose, and answers a question typed with one thumb. That is the
# judgement end of the work, not the procedure end — the opposite of the Ops trade, where a
# cheap model matching a runbook off a shelf is exactly right.
#
# IT IS ALSO THE EXPENSIVE CHOICE, AND KNOWINGLY. The account's five-hour window is Spira's
# real throughput ceiling and the concierge shares it with the operator's keyboard session,
# so a heavy concierge pass takes capacity from the builders. The brief tells the session to
# spend accordingly; the model is not where that economy is made, because a cheaper session
# that reaches the wrong conclusion about what to escalate costs far more than the tokens.
FAYTH_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5

# NO FAYTH_TOOLS, AND THAT IS THE DECLARATION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Every other persona
# names its tools because `aeon.sh` passes them to `--allowedTools`, which is an ALLOW LIST:
# naming tools is how a worker is kept inside its job. The concierge runs interactively under
# bypassed permissions, where the same flag can only ever SUBTRACT from what the operator can
# reach — so a list here could not widen the remit and could only narrow it by accident, on
# the day somebody adds a tool to the client and forgets this file.
#
# "Its remit is unbounded" (per the operator, 2026-08-30) is therefore expressed by the
# absence, and concierge.sh passes no --allowedTools at all. A maintained inventory that can
# only ever fall behind is the shape of the defect, not the guard against it.

# NO DEADLINE, NO LEASE, NO HEARTBEAT, NO CONCURRENCY CEILING — and every one of those
# absences is deliberate rather than unset.
#
# An aeon is bounded because it holds a bead: the wall is what returns the work to the queue
# when a session stops moving. The concierge holds nothing, so there is nothing to return,
# and a wall would only end a conversation the operator is in the middle of. It runs until it
# is stopped.
#
# FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT IS 1 AND THAT ONE IS NOT ENFORCED HERE — concierge.sh refuses to start
# a second tmux session of the same name, which is the mechanism. It is written down because
# "one session, not a repo-by-repo list" is a design decision worth reading, not an accident
# of how the launcher happens to work.
FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
